<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#feudalism">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feudalism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as semifedal, feudal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feudalism is a term for systems in which land was granted in return for military service and loyalty, creating hierarchies of lords and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Originally applied to medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, it is used more loosely for comparable arrangements elsewhere, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, and landholding in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feudalism.jpg" alt="Feudalism" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BNF_Fr_4274_8v_knight_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word feudal comes from the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> feudalis, from feudum, meaning fee or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, first attested in a charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> in 884. Its ultimate origin is unclear. People of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> did not conceive of feudalism as a formal political system. The noun feudalism was in use by the end of the 18th century.</p><p>Feudalism usually emerged from the decentralisation of an empire. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> of the 9th century, which lacked the bureaucratic infrastructure to support cavalry without allocating land to mounted troops, such soldiers began to secure hereditary rule over their lands, and their power extended to the social, political, judicial and economic spheres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The classic definition, by François Louis Ganshof in 1944, describes reciprocal legal and military obligations of the warrior nobility, built on lords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. A lord made someone his vassal at a commendation ceremony of homage and an oath of fealty, in which the vassal promised to fight at the lord's command and the lord to protect him. The vassal's principal obligation was aid or military service.</p><p>Marc Bloch's broader definition of 1939 includes the obligations of all three estates, the nobility, the clergy and the peasantry, who were bound by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manorialism">manorialism</a>, and is called feudal society. Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the term has been applied most often to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> and sometimes to medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and some scholars have used it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the 11th century saw what historians call a feudal revolution, a fragmentation of powers as castellans and lesser lords took over local rights. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> often pledged themselves to two or more lords, the idea of a liege lord, to whom obligations were superior, developed in the 12th century. Most military aspects of feudalism ended by about 1500, as armies became professional and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> weakened the nobility's hold over the lower classes. On the night of 4 August 1789 the French National Assembly announced that it abolished the feudal system entirely. Since Elizabeth A. R. Brown's The Tyranny of a Construct (1974) and Susan Reynolds's Fiefs and Vassals (1994) medieval historians have debated inconclusively whether feudalism is a useful construct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feudalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
